"""Additional uploaded papers and course-specific reading links. Called by content.py."""
from pathlib import Path
import json, math
BASE='https://mhrumi.github.io/Software-project-management/'
LESSONS=[('01-introduction.html','Introduction'),('02-project-evaluation.html','Project evaluation'),('03-project-planning.html','Step Wise planning'),('04-project-approach.html','Project approach'),('05a-estimation-concepts.html','Estimation concepts'),('05b-estimation-models.html','Estimation models'),('06-activity-planning.html','Activity planning'),('07-risk-management.html','Risk management'),('08-resource-allocation.html','Resource allocation'),('09-monitoring-control.html','Monitoring and control'),('10-managing-contracts.html','Contracts'),('11-managing-people.html','Managing people'),('12-working-in-teams.html','Teams and leadership'),('13-software-quality.html','Software quality')]
def extend(courses,course,add,spm,ml,sec,tt):
 for c in courses:c['session']='2020–21';c['family']=c['id'].split('-')[0];c['exam']='Final exam' if c is not tt else 'Term test'
 spm['materials']=[dict(title=t,url=BASE+n) for n,t in LESSONS]
 spm['refs'].insert(0,'User-provided SPM course materials: '+BASE)
 spm['note']+=' Course reading links are now available below. The course site does not define every term in this newer paper; those answers retain explicit interpretation notes.'
 tt['materials']=spm['materials']
 n=course('spm-2019','SPM · 2019–20','Software Project Management · 2019–20','CSE 421',[10,11,12,13],'June 2023 paper · session 2019–20. Every option in Groups A and B, with worked scheduling and investment calculations.', ['Four Ps: People, Product, Process, Project.','Risk exposure = probability × loss; expected monetary value = sum(probability × outcome).','EF = ES + duration; ES = maximum predecessor EF; float = LS − ES.','Net profit = sum of all net cash flows, including the negative initial investment.','Course ROI/ARR = (net profit / project life) / initial investment × 100%.','Interpolated payback = last negative year + unrecovered amount / next year cash flow, assuming uniform receipts.','Critical path for 3(b): B–E–G–I–L–M; duration 15 weeks.'],spm['refs'][:], 'Printed header: June 2023, session 2019–20; 2 hours, 50 marks. Overlapping photographs are preserved. All 34 options are answered. ROI uses the average-annual-profit convention in your course materials; payback timing assumptions are explicit.')
 n.update(session='2019–20',family='spm',exam='June 2023 final',materials=spm['materials'])
 def a(ref,title,body,final,lesson=None,figure=None):
  add(n,ref,title,body,final)
  if lesson:n['items'][-1]['sources']=[dict(title=dict(LESSONS).get(lesson,lesson),url=BASE+lesson)]
  if figure:n['items'][-1]['figure']=figure
 a('1(a)','The four Ps','People: stakeholders and the team who do and use the work. Product: objectives, scope, requirements and constraints. Process: the lifecycle and engineering practices used to build it. Project: planning, estimation, scheduling, monitoring and control. Example: for a library portal, librarians and developers are the people; borrowing functions define the product; iterative delivery is the process; the schedule and budget belong to project management.','People, Product, Process and Project.','01-introduction.html')
 a('1(b)','Feasibility study','A feasibility study checks whether a proposed system is practical and worthwhile before committing to full development. Assess technical capability, economic costs and benefits, operational fit, schedule and relevant legal constraints. Its recommendation is to proceed, revise or reject the proposal.','Check whether the proposal can and should be carried out.','02-project-evaluation.html')
 a('1(c)','Contract','In this project context, a contract is an enforceable agreement between customer and supplier setting out their obligations. It specifies deliverables, acceptance, price/payment, schedule and how changes, ownership, disputes and termination are handled.','An agreement defining enforceable customer and supplier obligations.','10-managing-contracts.html')
 a('1(d)','Cost–benefit analysis','Identify the expected costs and benefits of each project option, quantify them where reasonable and compare them over a stated period. Include development, operation and maintenance, as well as expected savings or income. Discount future cash flows when using NPV; acknowledge benefits that cannot credibly be monetized.','Compare expected project costs with expected benefits.','02-project-evaluation.html')
 a('1(e)','Cost flow forecasting','The paper says cost flow forecasting. It means estimating when project expenditure will occur, based on scheduled activities and resource use, so funding is available when needed. If the intended term is cash-flow forecasting, include both incoming and outgoing cash and calculate their net balance for each period.','Forecast the timing of costs; cash-flow forecasting includes receipts too.','08-resource-allocation.html')
 a('1(f)','Critical path','The critical path is the longest-duration dependency path from project start to finish. It fixes the earliest completion date in the precedence model. Its activities have zero total float under the usual earliest-finish schedule. A delay on an unchanged critical path delays project completion unless time is recovered elsewhere.','The longest-duration path, determining minimum project duration.','06-activity-planning.html')
 a('1(g)','Inputs to total software project cost','Estimate effort for each role and activity, multiply by the corresponding labor rate, and add hardware/software, services, facilities, travel/training and allocated overhead. Include the agreed contingency or risk allowance. Formula: total budget = sum(effort × labor rate) + nonlabor direct costs + overhead + contingency. State whether the estimate covers development only or the whole lifecycle; elapsed time alone is not effort.','Effort, labor rates, nonlabor costs, overhead and contingency.','08-resource-allocation.html')
 a('1(h)','Maslow’s hierarchy of needs','From lower to higher: physiological needs, safety, belonging/social needs, esteem and self-actualization. Workplace examples are reasonable pay and rest, job security, team inclusion, recognition, and opportunities for growth. This is a motivational framework, not a rigid rule that every person follows in exactly the same order.','Physiological → Safety → Belonging → Esteem → Self-actualization.','11-managing-people.html')
 a('2(a)','Why software projects are difficult','Invisibility: working progress is hard to see, so use demonstrable deliverables. Complexity: many interacting states and dependencies make prediction and testing difficult. Conformity: software must fit external rules, legacy systems and sometimes inconsistent requirements. Flexibility: because software can be changed, stakeholders may expect unlimited change; assess and control its effects on scope, cost and time.','Invisibility, complexity, conformity and flexibility.','01-introduction.html')
 a('2(b)','Project portfolio management','Portfolio management coordinates a set of projects so limited resources support organizational strategy. Portfolio definition establishes what work exists or is proposed, including unofficial work. Portfolio management prioritizes, resources, monitors and coordinates that work. Portfolio optimization adjusts the mix to balance strategic value, risk, capacity and short- versus long-term benefits; stop or defer weak proposals when justified.','Define the portfolio, manage it, then optimize its balance.','02-project-evaluation.html')
 a('2(c)','Decision trees for risk evaluation','Represent a choice as a decision node and its uncertain outcomes as chance branches. Assign probabilities and monetary outcomes, including relevant costs. Calculate EMV = sum(p_i × outcome_i) at each chance node and work backward to choose the preferable option. Example: a risky option gives +100,000 with probability 0.6 and −40,000 with probability 0.4, so EMV = 60,000 −16,000 = 44,000. A certain +35,000 has lower EMV, but a risk-averse decision maker may still prefer it. EMV is not a guaranteed outcome.','Compare probability-weighted outcomes by rolling back a decision tree.','02-project-evaluation.html', 'decision-risk.png')
 a('2(d)','Selecting the right person for a job','Define the role, tasks and essential competencies first. Write a job description and a person specification with justified selection criteria. Shortlist against those criteria, use a structured interview plus a relevant work sample, and check evidence/references with appropriate consent. Consider communication, teamwork, learning ability and availability alongside technical skills. Record a fair comparison, select the best fit and arrange induction/training; do not rely only on personal impressions.','Define requirements, gather job-relevant evidence, compare fairly and onboard.','11-managing-people.html')
 a('2(e)','Waterfall drawbacks and the spiral model','Waterfall proceeds through requirements, design, implementation, testing and delivery. Its weaknesses include expensive late changes, late user feedback and integration/testing problems discovered after substantial work. It suits stable, well-understood requirements better than highly uncertain ones.\nThe spiral model repeats a risk-driven cycle: establish objectives, alternatives and constraints; evaluate alternatives and resolve major risks, often through prototypes; develop and verify the next product level; review results and plan the next cycle.\nMerits: early attention to high risks, repeated stakeholder feedback, incremental commitment and accommodation of change. Demerits: risk analysis needs expertise, management overhead can be high, completion can be difficult to bound and it may be excessive for small low-risk work.','Waterfall handles change poorly; spiral uses repeated risk-driven cycles.','04-project-approach.html','spiral-cycle.png')
 a('2(f)','Managing activity risks','For each work package, identify what could prevent its completion: uncertain estimates, skills gaps, unavailable resources or dependency failure. Assess probability and schedule/cost impact; prioritize risks, especially on critical or near-critical paths. Assign an owner and choose avoidance, reduction, transfer or acceptance. Add justified contingency and trigger-based response actions to the plan. Review the risk log and revise estimates/dependencies as evidence changes. Example: prototype a risky migration early, retain a recovery plan and reserve time for data cleanup.','Identify, assess, assign, respond, reserve and monitor.','07-risk-management.html')
 a('3(a)','Activities covered by software project management','Feasibility study: establish the business need, possible solutions, expected benefits, costs and viability; decide whether to proceed. Planning: specify objectives, scope, products, activities, dependencies, resources, estimates, schedule, quality and risk responses. Project execution: coordinate development and delivery while monitoring actual progress, controlling changes and corrective action, and managing people/contracts. Close with acceptance, handover and lessons.\nThe diagram separates the management lifecycle from development tasks: requirements, design, implementation and testing happen within execution. Monitoring and control provide feedback that can revise the plan throughout the project.','Feasibility → Planning → Execution, with monitoring/control feedback and closure.','01-introduction.html','spm-lifecycle.png')
 durations=dict(A=1,B=2,C=2,D=4,E=1,F=2,G=3,H=1,I=4,J=1,K=3,L=4,M=1)
 pred=dict(A=[],B=[],C=['A'],D=['A'],E=['B'],F=['C','D'],G=['E'],H=['G'],I=['G'],J=['F'],K=['J','H'],L=['I'],M=['K','L'])
 es={};ef={}
 for k,d in durations.items():es[k]=max([ef[p] for p in pred[k]] or [0]);ef[k]=es[k]+d
 finish=max(ef.values());ls={};lf={}
 for k in reversed(durations):
  succ=[q for q in durations if k in pred[q]];lf[k]=min([ls[q] for q in succ] or [finish]);ls[k]=lf[k]-durations[k]
 rows=['Activity | duration | predecessors | ES | EF | LS | LF | float']+[f'{k} | {durations[k]} | {",".join(pred[k]) or "–"} | {es[k]} | {ef[k]} | {ls[k]} | {lf[k]} | {ls[k]-es[k]}' for k in durations]
 assert finish==15 and [k for k in durations if ls[k]==es[k]]==list('BEGILM')
 a('3(b)','Precedence diagram and critical path','Step 1: draw one node per activity and connect each predecessor to its successor. Start A and B together; finish only after M. Activity durations are in weeks.\nStep 2, forward pass: ES = maximum predecessor EF; EF = ES + duration. For example F waits for both C and D: ES(F)=max(3,5)=5, EF(F)=7. M waits for K and L: ES(M)=max(11,14)=14, EF(M)=15.\nStep 3, backward pass: set finish=15; LF = minimum successor LS, LS=LF−duration; float=LS−ES.\n'+'\n'.join(rows)+'\nStep 4: trace the connected zero-float activities B–E–G–I–L–M. Duration=2+1+3+4+4+1=15 weeks. A–D–F–J–K–M takes 12 weeks, so it does not control completion. Critical path assumes the printed dependencies and unlimited resource availability; resource constraints can change the schedule.','Critical path B–E–G–I–L–M; total duration 15 weeks.','06-activity-planning.html','spm-critical-path.png')
 n['network']=dict(durations=durations,pred=pred,es=es,ef=ef,ls=ls,lf=lf)
 a('3(c)','Stages of contract placement','1. Analyse requirements and define the required deliverables, quality and acceptance criteria. 2. Prepare an invitation to tender or request for proposals with scope, deadlines and evaluation criteria. 3. Identify suitable suppliers, issue the invitation and manage clarifications fairly. 4. Evaluate proposals using the stated technical, commercial, capability and risk criteria; shortlist and check evidence, demonstrations or references. 5. Negotiate scope, responsibilities, acceptance, payment, intellectual property and change procedures. 6. Award and sign the contract, notify bidders appropriately and hand it to contract management for monitoring. Placement ends with the agreement; ongoing supplier monitoring follows it.','Requirements → invitation → supplier proposals → evaluation → negotiation → award.','10-managing-contracts.html')
 a('4(a)','Simplest software development paradigm','Build-and-fix is the simplest informal paradigm: write an initial version, inspect it and repeatedly modify it until it seems satisfactory. It has little explicit requirements analysis, design or planning. This can be tolerable for a tiny personal experiment but gives weak control over larger projects. Waterfall is a simple structured sequential lifecycle; it is distinct from build-and-fix, which appears first in your course’s approach lesson.','Build-and-fix: build an initial version, then repeatedly repair it.','04-project-approach.html')
 a('4(b)','PRINCE2','PRINCE2 expands to PRojects IN Controlled Environments, version 2. It is a structured project-management method emphasizing continued business justification, defined roles, staged management and control by exception. It manages the project and can coexist with different software development lifecycles.','A structured, business-justified, stage-based project-management method.','03-project-planning.html')
 a('4(c)','Project stakeholders','Stakeholders are people, groups or organizations that affect the project, are affected by it or have an interest in its outcomes. Examples include sponsor, customer, end users, project team, supplier and regulator. Identify their needs, influence and communication requirements.','Those who affect, are affected by or have an interest in the project.','01-introduction.html')
 a('4(d)','Data mining for credit-card fraud detection','For unusual transactions without reliable fraud labels, use anomaly/outlier detection: flag transactions that deviate from normal behavior. When historical transactions have fraud/nonfraud labels, supervised classification can estimate fraud risk. For this short question, anomaly detection is the usual expected task; the correct modeling choice depends on available labels.','Anomaly detection; supervised classification is suitable when labels exist.')
 a('4(e)','Bespoke system','A bespoke system is designed or adapted specifically for one customer’s requirements, rather than sold as an unchanged general-purpose package. Example: custom admission-processing software that implements a university’s exact rules and integrations.','Custom-built software for a particular customer.','01-introduction.html')
 a('4(f)','Requirements analysis','Requirements analysis examines stakeholder needs to clarify, organize, model, check and agree what the system must do and the constraints it must satisfy. Resolve conflicts and ambiguities, prioritize requirements and define verifiable acceptance criteria. It includes functional and nonfunctional needs.','Clarify and validate agreed functional needs and quality constraints.','01-introduction.html')
 a('4(g)','Work Breakdown Structure','A WBS hierarchically decomposes the complete project scope into deliverables and manageable work packages. It supports estimation, ownership and control. The child elements should collectively cover the parent scope without overlap. Dependencies and dates belong in the associated schedule, not in the WBS alone.','A hierarchical decomposition of all project work.','06-activity-planning.html')
 a('4(h)','Payback period','The payback period is the time required for accumulated net cash inflows to recover the initial investment. It measures recovery speed, not total lifetime profit. Ordinary payback ignores the time value of money and cash flows after recovery; discounted payback is a different measure.','Time to recover the original investment from net cash inflows.','02-project-evaluation.html')
 a('5(a)','Egoless programming','Egoless programming separates a programmer’s self-worth from their code. Treat defects as issues to improve, welcome peer review, share knowledge and let the team own the product. Critique the work respectfully rather than blame the author; accept evidence and revise a design when a better one is found. It encourages review and collective learning without eliminating individual accountability.','Review code without personal defensiveness; improve it collaboratively.','12-working-in-teams.html')
 a('5(b)','Hackman–Oldham job characteristics model','The paper’s “Oldhun-Hackman” refers to Hackman and Oldham. Five core job dimensions are skill variety, task identity, task significance, autonomy and feedback. The first three support experienced meaningfulness; autonomy supports responsibility; feedback gives knowledge of results. These psychological states can promote intrinsic motivation, satisfaction and effective work, moderated by individual and contextual differences.\nMotivating Potential Score: MPS=[(skill variety + task identity + task significance)/3] × autonomy × feedback, using compatible rating scales. Example: give a developer ownership of a complete user-facing feature, freedom over implementation within constraints, and direct feedback about how it works.\nThe supplied Week 11 page does not explicitly cover this model. Supplemental primary reference: Hackman & Oldham, Motivation through the design of work: test of a theory (1976), DOI 10.1016/0030-5073(76)90016-7.','Five dimensions support meaningfulness, responsibility and knowledge of results.')
 n['items'][-1]['sources']=[dict(title='Hackman & Oldham (1976), original study',url='https://doi.org/10.1016/0030-5073(76)90016-7')]
 a('5(c)','Objectives of activity planning','Identify the work needed to deliver the agreed products; establish dependencies and a feasible sequence; estimate duration and resource needs; allocate responsibility; identify critical activities and risks; and create a schedule/baseline for monitoring and control. It also checks whether deadlines and resource limits are achievable before committing to them.','Identify, sequence, estimate and resource work, then establish a controllable schedule.','06-activity-planning.html')
 a('5(d)','Factors making a job meaningful','In the Hackman–Oldham model, experienced meaningfulness is supported by skill variety (using different skills), task identity (completing an identifiable whole piece of work) and task significance (making a worthwhile difference to others). For example, building and validating an entire accessible booking feature uses several skills, has a visible result and helps real users. Autonomy and feedback matter too, but map primarily to responsibility and knowledge of results.','Skill variety, task identity and task significance.')
 a('5(e)','Fixed-price contract: advantages and disadvantages','The supplier agrees a price for a defined scope, subject to the agreed change terms. Customer advantages: greater initial budget predictability, easier bid comparison and more cost-overrun risk allocated to the supplier. Supplier advantages: clear commercial target and potential gain from efficiency.\nDisadvantages: a precise scope is needed; uncertainty may produce a large risk premium; changes require negotiation and can trigger disputes; a supplier under pressure may cut corners unless quality and acceptance are explicit. The supplier bears estimation/overrun risk. A fixed price does not mean every later scope change is free.','Budget clarity and efficiency incentives, but scope rigidity, risk premiums and change disputes.','10-managing-contracts.html')
 a('5(f)','Risk assessment and formula','Risk assessment identifies and analyzes uncertain events to prioritize response. A quantitative expected-loss measure is risk exposure RE=P(event) × loss if it occurs. Example: probability 0.2 and loss 50,000 gives RE=10,000 in expected monetary loss. For a probability-impact matrix, ordinal category scores can be multiplied for prioritization, but the result is a ranking score rather than a literal currency expectation. Also inspect high-impact risks that a simple average can obscure.','Risk exposure = probability × consequence (expected loss when quantified).','07-risk-management.html')
 a('6(a)','Leadership models and leader functions','Style models distinguish autocratic/directive decisions, democratic/participative decisions and laissez-faire/delegated working. Their suitability depends on urgency, competence and coordination needs; delegation still requires accountability.\nThe course’s situational model tailors the approach to the specific task: directing gives a novice clear steps; coaching adds explanation and feedback as competence grows; supporting shares decisions when capability is high but confidence/motivation needs help; delegating gives capable, committed people responsibility within agreed goals.\nLeader functions include setting direction, organizing responsibilities, communicating priorities, motivating and developing people, resolving conflict, removing obstacles, representing the team and monitoring outcomes. Example: during a first production release, guide the junior through a checklist, pair them with an experienced colleague and progressively delegate as they demonstrate competence.','Choose a suitable style; provide direction, support, coordination and accountability.','12-working-in-teams.html')
 a('6(b)','Step Wise project planning','0. Select the project: confirm business justification. 1. Identify scope and objectives: state deliverables and success criteria. 2. Identify project infrastructure: standards, roles, tools and reporting. 3. Analyze project characteristics: choose an appropriate development approach. 4. Identify products and activities: define deliverables, work and dependencies. 5. Estimate activity effort/duration. 6. Identify activity risks and response needs. 7. Allocate resources and produce a feasible schedule. 8. Review and publicize the plan with stakeholder agreement. 9. Execute the plan and monitor it. 10. Perform lower-level planning as activities approach, revisiting products, estimates and risks.\nPlanning is iterative: insufficient resources can require revised estimates, scope or sequencing, followed by review and approval. Your course diagram separates execution and lower-level planning, while its prose combines them under step 9; both operations are included here.','Select → objectives → infrastructure → characteristics → products/activities → estimates → risks → resources → review → execute and replan.','03-project-planning.html')
 flows=[[-100000,10000,10000,20000,20000,100000],[-1000000,200000,200000,200000,200000,350000],[-100000,30000,30000,30000,20000,20000],[-120000,30000,30000,30000,25000,50000]]
 lines=['Use the printed question table, not a similar textbook table: Project 1 year 3 is 20,000, and Project 3 year 5 is 20,000. All values use the table’s unstated currency unit.','Step 1: net profit = total positive inflows − initial investment. Step 2: compute cumulative cash flow until investment is recovered. Step 3: use the course’s annual ROI/ARR = (net profit / 5) / initial investment × 100%.']
 results=[]
 for idx,f in enumerate(flows,1):
  initial=-f[0];net=sum(f);cum=[];s=0
  for x in f:s+=x;cum.append(s)
  j=next(k for k in range(1,len(cum)) if cum[k]>=0);pay=(j-1)+(-cum[j-1])/f[j];roi=net/5/initial*100
  results.append(dict(project=idx,net=net,payback=pay,roi=roi,cumulative=cum))
  lines.append(f'Project {idx}: inflows={sum(f[1:]):,}; net profit={sum(f[1:]):,}−{initial:,}={net:,}. Cumulative at years 0–5: '+', '.join(f'{x:,}' for x in cum)+f'. Payback={j-1}+{abs(cum[j-1]):,}/{f[j]:,}={pay:.4f} years. Average annual profit={net:,}/5={net/5:,.0f}; ROI={net/5:,.0f}/{initial:,}×100={roi:.2f}%.')
 lines+=['Summary: Project 1: profit 60,000; payback 4.4000 years; annual ROI 12%. Project 2: profit 150,000; payback 4.5714 years; annual ROI 3%. Project 3: profit 30,000; payback 3.5000 years; annual ROI 6%. Project 4: profit 45,000; payback 4.1000 years; annual ROI 7.5%.','Payback interpolation assumes the next year’s inflow arrives evenly. If all annual flows occur only at each year-end, payback is at year 5 for Projects 1, 2 and 4, and year 4 for Project 3. The materials use interpolation, so that is the main answer.','Cumulative lifetime return net profit/investment is 60%, 15%, 30% and 37.5%, respectively; these are not the annual ROI values requested under the course convention. Highest absolute profit: Project 2. Fastest payback: Project 3. Highest annual ROI: Project 1.']
 a('6(c)','Net profit, payback and ROI: all four projects','\n'.join(lines),'P1: 60,000; 4.4 y; 12%. P2: 150,000; 4.5714 y; 3%. P3: 30,000; 3.5 y; 6%. P4: 45,000; 4.1 y; 7.5%.','02-project-evaluation.html')
 n['financial_results']=results
 # Preserve course specificity: source links guide reading, rather than claim the page supplies an official answer key.
 mapping={'1':'01-introduction.html','2':'03-project-planning.html','3':'05b-estimation-models.html','4':'07-risk-management.html','5':'13-software-quality.html','6':'13-software-quality.html'}
 for it in spm['items']:
  lesson=mapping[it['ref'][0]]
  if it['ref'] in ['4(a)','4(f)','5(a)','6(b)']:lesson='13-software-quality.html'
  elif it['ref'] in ['5(b)','5(c)','6(a)']:lesson='03-project-planning.html'
  elif it['ref'] in ['2(d)','3(c)','4(c)','4(d)','4(g)','5(d)','6(c)']:lesson='07-risk-management.html'
  elif it['ref']=='2(e)':lesson='05b-estimation-models.html'
  if it['ref'] in ['1(f)']:lesson='13-software-quality.html'
  elif it['ref'] in ['1(c)']:lesson='04-project-approach.html'
  elif it['ref'] in ['1(e)','1(g)']:lesson='03-project-planning.html'
  elif it['ref'] in ['2(a)','2(b)','2(f)']:lesson='01-introduction.html'
  if it['ref'] not in ['2(c)','4(e)']:
   it['sources']=[dict(title='Related course reading · '+dict(LESSONS)[lesson],url=BASE+lesson)]
 spm['refs'] += ['SPM reading index (all lessons): '+BASE]
 assert len(n['items'])==34
 return n

def extend_ml(courses,course,add,old):
 import copy
 c=course('ml-2019','ML · 2019–20','Machine Learning · 2019–20','CSE 475',[14,15,16,17],'June 2024 paper · session 2019–20. All options, including entropy, spam metrics, neural-network expressions and convolution.',old['formula']+['Entropy H = −sum(p log2 p); information gain = parent entropy − weighted child entropy.','Valid convolution size = floor((input − kernel)/stride)+1 per axis.'],old['refs']+['Keras model training API: https://keras.io/api/models/model_training_apis/','Nested CV example: https://scikit-learn.org/stable/auto_examples/model_selection/plot_nested_cross_validation_iris.html'], 'Printed header: June 2024, session 2019–20; 3 hours, 100 marks. Every listed option is covered. The paper’s wording about logistic regression and SVM is clarified rather than repeated as a false claim.')
 c.update(session='2019–20',family='ml',exam='June 2024 final')
 def a(ref,title,body,final,fig=None):
  add(c,ref,title,body,final)
  if fig:c['items'][-1]['figure']=fig
 def reuse(ref,oldref,title=None):
  i=copy.deepcopy(next(i for i in old['items'] if i['ref']==oldref));i['ref']=ref
  if title:i['title']=title
  c['items'].append(i)
 a('1(a)','Plurality voting','Each classifier or neighbor votes for a class; choose the class with the most votes. It does not require more than half of all votes. Example: A=4, B=3, C=2; A wins with 4 of 9 votes. Define a tie rule in advance.','Choose the class receiving the largest number of votes.')
 a('1(b)','Two loss functions','Mean squared error: L=(1/n)sum(y−prediction)^2, commonly used for regression. Binary cross-entropy: L=−(1/n)sum[y log(p)+(1−y)log(1−p)], used for binary classification probabilities. Loss measures prediction error to guide fitting.','MSE and binary cross-entropy.')
 reuse('1(c)','4(g)')
 a('1(d)','Bootstrap sample','Draw observations from the original dataset with replacement. A sample often has the same number of rows as the original, but repeated rows occur and some originals are absent. Example: from [A,B,C,D], one bootstrap sample is [B,B,D,A]. This is used in bagging.','A random sample drawn with replacement.')
 a('1(e)','Two reinforcement-learning examples','A robot learns navigation actions from rewards for reaching a goal and penalties for collisions. A game-playing agent learns moves from rewards for winning or increasing score. In both, actions affect future states and the objective is cumulative reward, rather than simply copying independent labeled examples.','Robot navigation and game-playing through interaction and rewards.')
 a('1(f)','When decision-tree entropy is zero','H=−sum p_k log2(p_k). If every observation belongs to one class, its probability is 1 and the others are 0, so H=−1 log2(1)=0, using the convention 0 log2(0)=0. This is a pure node.','Entropy is zero when the node contains only one class.')
 a('1(g)','High bias','High bias is systematic error from overly restrictive assumptions. A model too simple for the true pattern may underfit, performing poorly on both training and validation data. Example: fitting a straight line to a strongly curved relationship. Increase suitable model capacity, improve features or reduce excessive regularization.','An overly restrictive model systematically underfits the pattern.')
 a('2(a)','Explain model.compile and model.fit','compile configures learning: optimizer="rmsprop" chooses the RMSprop update method; loss="binary_crossentropy" compares binary labels with output probabilities; metrics=["accuracy"] requests reported classification accuracy. compile itself does not train the weights.\nfit(x_train,y_train,epochs=4,batch_size=512) trains on the examples and labels for four passes through the training set, normally updating after each mini-batch of up to 512 examples. With N examples and the last partial batch retained, each epoch has ceiling(N/512) updates. N is not supplied, so a numerical count cannot be determined. The model needs a suitable binary output; accuracy is a reporting metric, not the loss optimized here.','Configure RMSprop, binary cross-entropy and accuracy; train 4 epochs with batch size 512.')
 c['items'][-1]['sources']=[dict(title='Keras training API',url='https://keras.io/api/models/model_training_apis/')]
 a('2(b)','Perceptron limitation and logistic regression','A single perceptron learns a linear separating boundary and is guaranteed to converge only when data are linearly separable under the usual assumptions. With overlapping classes it can keep changing weights without finding a perfect separator; its hard step output is not a calibrated probability.\nLogistic regression uses p(y=1|x)=sigmoid(w·x+b), a smooth cross-entropy objective and probabilistic outputs. It can fit a best-compromise linear classifier to overlapping data with regularization. Important correction: ordinary logistic regression still has a linear decision boundary in its input features; it does not by itself solve XOR or arbitrary nonlinear separation. Use nonlinear features, kernels or multilayer models when needed.','Logistic regression gives smooth probabilistic fitting; both ordinary models have linear boundaries.')
 a('2(c)','Dimensionality reduction and PCA compression','Reducing dimensions can reduce storage/computation, remove redundant directions, improve visualization and sometimes reduce noise/overfitting. It may also discard predictive information.\nPCA steps: split data; fit training means and scale features if their units require it; center the training matrix X; obtain principal directions from its covariance eigendecomposition or SVD; sort by decreasing variance; retain k directions. Compressed representation Z=X_centered W_k. Reconstruction X_hat=Z W_k^T+mean. When k<original dimension, reconstruction loses variation in discarded directions. Choose k using explained variance and task validation, and reuse the training transformation on test data. PCA maximizes retained variance, not class separation.','PCA projects centered data onto k high-variance orthogonal directions.')
 reuse('2(d)','2(d)')
 a('2(e)','Find the most discriminative feature','A discriminative feature helps distinguish target classes. On training data, compare each feature’s association with the class using an appropriate measure: information gain for categorical splits, or a justified statistical score for numerical inputs. Validate usefulness using a model and held-out or cross-validated permutation importance.\nFor information gain: compute parent class entropy, partition by a feature, calculate weighted child entropy and subtract it. Larger gain means greater impurity reduction for that split. Use identical evaluation conditions for all candidates and avoid test-set leakage. Correlated features and high-cardinality splits can mislead simple rankings; no dataset is supplied here, so no particular feature can be named.','Compare label-separation scores and validate predictive contribution without test leakage.')
 a('2(f)','SVM slack variables and nonlinear separation','For labels y_i∈{−1,+1}, soft-margin SVM minimizes (1/2)||w||²+C sum ξ_i subject to y_i(w·φ(x_i)+b)≥1−ξ_i and ξ_i≥0. ξ=0 meets the margin; 0<ξ<1 is inside it but correctly classified; ξ>1 is on the wrong side (ξ=1 lies at the decision boundary).\nSlack permits overlap or noisy points at a penalty; large C penalizes violations more strongly. Slack alone does not create a nonlinear boundary. A nonlinear feature map φ, typically computed through a kernel, supplies nonlinear separation in the original input space.','Slack permits penalized margin violations; kernels or nonlinear features handle nonlinear boundaries.')
 a('3(a)','Spam confusion matrix and all metrics','Take Spam as positive. True positives: rows 2,4,8 → TP=3. False negatives: rows 1,9 → FN=2. False positive: row 5 → FP=1. True negatives: rows 3,6,7,10 → TN=4.\nMatrix with actual rows and predicted columns [Spam, Not Spam]: actual Spam [3,2]; actual Not Spam [1,4]. Total=10. Accuracy=(TP+TN)/10=(3+4)/10=0.70=70%. Sensitivity/recall=TP/(TP+FN)=3/5=0.60=60%. Precision=TP/(TP+FP)=3/4=0.75=75%. F1=2TP/(2TP+FP+FN)=6/9=0.6667≈66.67%.','TP=3, FN=2, FP=1, TN=4; accuracy 70%, recall 60%, precision 75%, F1 66.67%.')
 reuse('3(b)(i)','2(f)')
 reuse('3(b)(ii)','2(b)')
 reuse('3(c)(i)','3(c)(i)')
 a('3(c)(ii)','Derive the network output','There are four input features, two neurons in hidden layer 1 and two in hidden layer 2. The small green z and a circles show preactivation and postactivation of the same neuron; they are not separate fully connected layers. Use column-vector convention, with w^(l)_{ji} connecting input i to output j.\nz1^(2)=sum(i=1..4) w^(1)_{1i} x_i+b1^(1); a1^(2)=g1(z1^(2)). z2^(2)=sum(i=1..4) w^(1)_{2i} x_i+b2^(1); a2^(2)=g1(z2^(2)).\nz1^(3)=w^(2)_{11}a1^(2)+w^(2)_{12}a2^(2)+b1^(2); a1^(3)=g2(z1^(3)). z2^(3)=w^(2)_{21}a1^(2)+w^(2)_{22}a2^(2)+b2^(2); a2^(3)=g2(z2^(3)).\nThe diagram’s final node is s with weights W^(3), and no output bias or activation is specified. Thus the direct weighted-sum interpretation is s=w^(3)_{11}a1^(3)+w^(3)_{12}a2^(3). If your lecture defines an output activation, write y=g_out(s); if it defines an output bias, add b_out explicitly.\nIn vector form without an extra output bias: s=W^(3) g2(W^(2) g1(W^(1)x+b^(1))+b^(2)). Shapes: W^(1):2×4; b^(1):2×1; W^(2):2×2; b^(2):2×1; W^(3):1×2. Weights/activations are unspecified, so this is a symbolic result.','s=W3·g2(W2·g1(W1·x+b1)+b2), with output activation/bias only if explicitly assumed.')
 reuse('4(a)','1(g)')
 a('4(b)','Nominal and ordinal features','Nominal values name categories without an inherent order: blood group A/B/AB/O or color red/green/blue. Ordinal values have a meaningful order but not necessarily equal spacing: satisfaction low/medium/high. Do not invent a numerical distance between nominal categories.','Nominal: blood group; ordinal: low/medium/high satisfaction.')
 a('4(c)','Batch gradient descent versus SGD','Batch gradient descent computes one gradient using the entire training set before updating weights: θ←θ−η∇J_all(θ). It gives a stable full-data direction but each update may be expensive. Stochastic gradient descent uses one sampled observation per update, giving cheaper but noisy steps. Mini-batch gradient descent uses a small group and is the usual practical compromise. Libraries sometimes use “SGD” broadly for mini-batch training.','Batch: all examples per update; strict SGD: one example per update.')
 reuse('4(d)','4(d)');reuse('4(e)','5(e)','Why k-NN is a lazy learner');reuse('4(f)','4(f)')
 a('4(g)','Metrics for imbalanced classification','Inspect class-wise precision and recall, F1 or F-beta when recall/precision emphasis matters, and precision-recall curves/average precision for rare-positive ranking tasks. Balanced accuracy averages recalls across classes. Report the confusion matrix and threshold, and choose according to false-positive/false-negative costs. Ordinary accuracy alone can conceal failure on the minority class.','Use class-wise precision/recall, F1, PR analysis and balanced accuracy as appropriate.')
 a('5(a)','Variables of an artificial neuron','x1…xN are input features. w1…wN are learned weights controlling contribution. b is the learned bias shifting the decision threshold. Σ combines inputs: z=sum(w_i x_i)+b. σ is an activation function, which may be sigmoid, ReLU or another specified function. Output y=σ(z). For sigmoid, σ(z)=1/(1+e^(−z)); do not assume every σ label means sigmoid unless the lecture defines it that way.','Inputs x, weights w, bias b, weighted sum z and activated output y.')
 a('5(b)','Nested k-fold CV: selection and tuning','The outer loop estimates generalization of the whole selection procedure. Hold one outer fold out as a test set. Using only the remaining outer training data, run an inner CV search over candidate model families and hyperparameters. Fit preprocessing inside each inner training fold too.\nChoose the best inner-validation configuration, refit it on all outer training data and score it once on the untouched outer test fold. Repeat for every outer fold and summarize outer scores. Do not tune on those outer test scores. After evaluating the procedure, run the same inner selection on all development data and refit a final model; use an independent final test only if reserved. The diagram illustrates one outer split and its inner search.','Inner CV selects models/settings; outer CV evaluates the complete selection procedure.','nested-cv.png')
 c['items'][-1]['sources']=[dict(title='scikit-learn nested CV example',url='https://scikit-learn.org/stable/auto_examples/model_selection/plot_nested_cross_validation_iris.html')]
 a('5(c)','One-hot encoding a nominal feature','For color categories [red,green,blue], create binary columns [is_red,is_green,is_blue]. Red becomes [1,0,0], green [0,1,0], blue [0,0,1]. This avoids falsely treating category codes as ordered numbers. Fit the category vocabulary using training data and declare how unknown values are handled.\nMain disadvantage: high cardinality can create many sparse columns, increasing dimension and processing/storage demands. For an unregularized linear model with an intercept, all category columns are also linearly dependent, so one reference category may be dropped.','Replace each category with a binary indicator; many categories can create high dimension.')
 a('5(d)','k-means algorithm and flowchart','Choose k and initialize k centroids, preferably with a suitable seeding method. Assign each observation to its nearest centroid under the selected distance. Recompute each centroid as the mean of its assigned points. Repeat assignment and update until assignments stabilize, centroid movement is sufficiently small or a maximum iteration count is reached.\nThe objective is within-cluster sum of squared Euclidean distances. Initialization can affect the local solution; use multiple starts. Scale features where appropriate and define how empty clusters are reinitialized. The diagram shows the iterative assignment/update loop.','Initialize → assign nearest cluster → update means → repeat until convergence.','kmeans-flow.png')
 a('5(e)','Max-pooling in a CNN','Max-pooling takes the maximum within each local window, typically reducing spatial dimensions and computation while retaining strong activations. It provides some tolerance to small local shifts, but loses spatial detail and is not universal invariance.\nExample with 2×2 windows and stride 2: input rows [1,3,2,0]; [4,2,5,1]; [0,1,2,3]; [2,0,1,6]. Maxima are 4 for the top-left window, 5 top-right, 2 bottom-left and 6 bottom-right. Output rows are [4,5]; [2,6]. Pool each channel separately; ordinary max-pooling has no learned weights.','2×2 stride-2 pooling turns the example into [[4,5],[2,6]].')
 a('5(f)','Why k-NN is nonparametric and how ties are resolved','k-NN does not fit a fixed finite-dimensional functional form such as one straight line. It retains examples, and its effective decision complexity can grow with the training set; hence nonparametric. It still has hyperparameters such as k and distance metric.\nIf class votes tie, use a declared deterministic rule, such as distance-weighted voting, the class of the closest tied neighbor or a fixed class order. Odd k reduces binary-class vote ties but does not remove all multiclass or equal-distance ties.','No fixed fitted function form; declare a distance-based or deterministic tie rule.')
 a('6(a)(i)','Entropy and first split: X, Y or Z?','Rows are (X,Y,Z,C): (1,1,1,I), (1,1,0,I), (0,0,1,II), (1,0,0,II). Parent has two I and two II, so H(parent)=1 bit.\nSplit X: X=0 has one II (H=0); X=1 has two I and one II, H=−(2/3)log2(2/3)−(1/3)log2(1/3)=0.918296. Weighted H=(1/4)0+(3/4)0.918296=0.688722; IG(X)=0.311278.\nSplit Y: Y=1 contains two I; Y=0 contains two II. Both are pure, weighted H=0; IG(Y)=1.\nSplit Z: Z=1 has one I and one II, and Z=0 has one I and one II. Both have H=1; weighted H=1; IG(Z)=0.\nChoose Y: it gives the largest information gain and perfectly separates these four training rows.','IG(X)=0.311278, IG(Y)=1, IG(Z)=0; split first on Y.')
 a('6(a)(ii)','Bagging ensemble classifier','Create multiple bootstrap samples of the training set. Fit a separate base learner to each sample, usually independently. Combine classification outputs by majority/plurality voting or probability averaging; regression uses averaging. The diversity reduces variance when base learners are unstable and not perfectly correlated. Random forests add random feature selection to tree bagging. Out-of-bag cases can support internal evaluation; a held-out test remains separate.','Bootstrap training sets → fit independent learners → aggregate their predictions.')
 a('6(b)(i)','Learning rate: too small, suitable, too large','Gradient descent updates θ_new=θ_old−η∇J(θ_old); η is the learning rate controlling step size. A very small η takes tiny steps and converges slowly; a suitable η reduces loss efficiently; an excessive η overshoots and can oscillate or diverge.\nFor J(w)=w²/2, gradient=w and w_new=(1−η)w. With w0=4: η=0.1 gives 4,3.6,3.24,…; η=1 gives 4,0,0,…; η=2.2 gives 4,−4.8,5.76,… and diverges. These exact examples illustrate the principle, not universal learning-rate recommendations. The figure shows loss across iterations for the three cases.','Learning rate controls step size; too small is slow and too large can diverge.','learning-rate.png')
 a('6(b)(ii)','Interpret training/validation accuracy versus C','In the common logistic-regression convention in this plot, C is inverse regularization strength. At very small C, strong regularization limits the model, so both training and validation accuracy are relatively low: underfitting. As C increases, both improve. Validation accuracy reaches its best plotted region around C=1; choose settings using validation or CV, not training accuracy.\nAt larger C, training accuracy stays high or rises, while validation accuracy falls and the gap widens: overfitting with weaker regularization. The graph is qualitative; do not claim exact accuracy decimals from the photograph. C≈1 is the best visible candidate among those plotted, not a universal optimum.','Small C underfits; around C=1 validates best; large C weakens regularization and overfits.')
 a('6(c)(i)','Why CNNs suit image classification','Local receptive fields exploit nearby pixel structure; shared convolution weights detect the same feature across locations using far fewer parameters than a fully connected image layer. Stacked layers build spatial feature hierarchies. Convolution is translation-equivariant away from boundary effects, while pooling/aggregation can provide some shift tolerance; a CNN is not automatically invariant to every shift or distortion.','Local connectivity and shared filters exploit spatial structure efficiently.')
 a('6(c)(ii)','3×3 convolution on a 5×5 input, stride 2','Assume no padding (valid convolution), since none is specified. Output height=width=floor((5−3)/2)+1=2, so one filter extracts 2×2=4 patches. Zero-based top-left coordinates are (0,0), (0,2), (2,0), (2,2). Each patch is 3×3×1; multiply it elementwise by the shared kernel and sum, adding a bias if defined.\nNo input or kernel values are given, so numerical feature values cannot be computed. With “same” padding, output size would instead be ceiling(5/2)=3 per axis, or 9 padded patches. The valid assumption is therefore essential.','With valid/no padding: 2×2 output, 4 patches; same padding would give 9.')
 for ref,fig in [('3(c)(ii)','ml-network-original.jpg'),('5(a)','ml-neuron-original.jpg'),('6(b)(ii)','ml-validation-original.jpg')]:
  next(i for i in c['items'] if i['ref']==ref)['figure']=fig
 return c

def extend_security(courses,course,add,old):
 import copy
 c=course('security-2023','Security · 2023','Computer Security & Forensics · 2023','CSE 461',[18,19,20],'Additional 2023 paper. All question options, original scans, worked AES, RSA, ElGamal and birthday-probability calculations.',['Birthday collision P=1−product(i=0..n−1)(1−i/N).','AES state uses column-major byte order; MixColumns uses GF(2^8), not ordinary integer addition.','RSA n=pq; phi=(p−1)(q−1); ed≡1 mod phi; C=M^e mod n.','ElGamal y=g^x mod p; c1=g^k; c2=M y^k; M=c2/(c1^x), modulo p.','2-byte salt has 2^16=65,536 possible values; known-salt targeting differs from all-salt precomputation.'],old['refs']+['AES FIPS 197: https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/197/final','NIST forensic guide: https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/86/final','DNSSEC RFC 4033: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4033'], 'The printed Security header says 2023 but does not show a session. It is not relabelled as session 2019–20. All listed options are addressed. Educational descriptions of attacks are paired with defensive context; no live system is targeted.')
 c.update(session='Not printed',family='security',exam='2023 final')
 def a(ref,title,body,final,fig=None):
  add(c,ref,title,body,final)
  if fig:c['items'][-1]['figure']=fig
 def reuse(ref,oldref,title=None):
  i=copy.deepcopy(next(i for i in old['items'] if i['ref']==oldref));i['ref']=ref
  if title:i['title']=title
  c['items'].append(i)
 a('1(a)','Confidentiality and four controls','Confidentiality prevents information being disclosed to unauthorized parties. Four supporting controls are encryption, authentication, access-control/authorization rules and physical protection. Example: encrypt a student database, require verified user identities, restrict records by role and secure the server location. Authentication identifies the user; authorization decides what that user can read.','Prevent unauthorized disclosure using encryption, authentication, authorization and physical controls.')
 a('1(b)','Biometrics and four candidates','Biometric authentication uses measurable physical or behavioral characteristics to help recognize a person. Candidates include fingerprint, iris pattern, facial features and voice characteristics. Enrollment stores a protected reference/template and later samples are compared against it. Performance depends on false acceptance/rejection and resistance to spoofing; a biometric is not a secret that can simply be changed like a password.','Fingerprint, iris, face and voice are biometric candidates.')
 a('1(c)','Possible attack on one-time-pad encryption','A correctly used OTP with a uniformly random secret key as long as the message, used exactly once, has perfect secrecy against ciphertext-only recovery. There is no generic brute-force method that uniquely identifies the true plaintext without additional information.\nA reused pad is vulnerable: C1 xor C2=P1 xor P2 exposes relations between messages. OTP also lacks integrity: a ciphertext bit flip causes the corresponding plaintext bit flip, so known-format messages can be modified without learning the key. Key theft or nonrandom pads break the assumptions.','Correct OTP resists plaintext recovery, but pad reuse, key compromise and unauthenticated modification are threats.')
 a('1(d)','Worm/malware lifecycle stages','A common classroom malware lifecycle is dormant (optional waiting), propagation (replication/spread), triggering (a condition activates the payload) and execution (the payload performs its action). Worm propagation includes discovering reachable targets and spreading through vulnerabilities or other channels. “Development steps” could instead mean a course-specific construction model; the uploaded question does not define one, so this answer states the lifecycle interpretation. Defensive controls include patching, isolation, least privilege and monitoring at each stage.','Dormant → propagation → triggering → execution, under the lifecycle interpretation.')
 a('1(e)','Why a switch is usually safer than a hub','A hub repeats incoming signals to other ports, so any attached device can observe traffic on that shared segment. A switch learns MAC locations and normally forwards known unicast frames only to the relevant port, reducing casual interception. This is an improvement, not guaranteed confidentiality: broadcasts and unknown unicasts can be flooded, and ARP spoofing or configuration errors still threaten traffic. Use encryption and switch protections too.','Switches reduce unnecessary unicast exposure; hubs repeat traffic to all ports.')
 a('1(f)','Preventing ARP spoofing','Enable Dynamic ARP Inspection using trusted DHCP-snooping bindings so inconsistent ARP claims are rejected. For small fixed environments, carefully maintained static ARP bindings can protect critical peers, though they are difficult to scale. Restrict untrusted access ports and segment networks. TLS or VPN encryption reduces what an interceptor can read, but does not itself validate ARP messages. The question’s “two reasons” is interpreted as two preventive measures.','Use Dynamic ARP Inspection/DHCP snooping and appropriate static bindings or port controls.')
 a('1(g)','Symmetric keys in server-only communication','If N client users communicate only with one additional central server, give each client one unique shared key with the server. That requires N different keys, not N(N−1)/2, because client-to-client encrypted links are not required. If N counts all nodes including the server, the number would be N−1; state the interpretation.','N client users plus a server require N distinct client–server shared keys.')
 a('1(h)','Second-preimage versus collision resistance','Second-preimage resistance: given one particular message m, it should be infeasible to find a different m′ with H(m′)=H(m). Collision resistance: it should be infeasible to find any distinct pair m,m′ with equal hashes; neither is fixed in advance. For an ideal n-bit hash, generic work is about 2^n for second preimages and 2^(n/2) for collisions, subject to the usual idealized assumptions.','Second preimage fixes one message; collision search chooses both messages.')
 a('2(a)','Ten security principles','1. Economy of mechanism: keep the trusted design small and understandable. 2. Fail-safe defaults: deny access unless it is explicitly granted. 3. Complete mediation: check authorization for every relevant access. 4. Open design: rely on protected keys, not secrecy of the design. 5. Separation of privilege: require independent conditions for sensitive access.\n6. Least privilege: grant only the permissions needed. 7. Least common mechanism: minimize shared mechanisms that can leak or couple users. 8. Psychological acceptability: make secure use understandable and practical. 9. Work factor: ensure bypass costs are sufficiently high relative to attacker resources. 10. Compromise recording: make successful or attempted misuse detectable and attributable through protected audit records.\nThis is the classic eight design principles plus the two additional considerations often taught with them; the paper itself does not enumerate its intended list.','Small design, deny by default, check access, open design, separate privilege, least privilege, limit sharing, usability, work factor and auditability.')
 a('2(b)','Confusion and diffusion in AES','Confusion makes the relationship between key and ciphertext complex; AES SubBytes provides nonlinear substitution and supports confusion. Diffusion spreads each input’s influence across multiple output positions; ShiftRows permutes byte positions and MixColumns combines bytes, together spreading differences across rounds. AddRoundKey combines state and round-key bytes by XOR; it injects key dependence but alone is linear and does not supply all the cipher’s confusion.','SubBytes supports confusion; ShiftRows plus MixColumns support diffusion.')
 a('2(c)','Computer forensics and forensic paradigm','Computer forensics systematically preserves and examines digital data to establish defensible findings about events. A practical paradigm is collection/preservation, examination, analysis and reporting. Collect with authorization and maintain chain of custody; acquire appropriately verified copies, examining copies when possible. Extract relevant artifacts; correlate them and assess alternative explanations; then report methods, evidence, limitations and conclusions. Record timestamps, tool actions and hashes so others can assess integrity and reproduce the reasoning.','Preserve/collect → examine → analyze → report, with integrity and chain of custody.')
 a('2(d)','Buffer overflow and conceptual stack layout','A buffer overflow occurs when a program writes beyond a buffer’s allocated bounds. In a vulnerable stack frame, an unchecked write may corrupt adjacent local variables, control data or a saved return address. The diagram is conceptual: exact order, growth direction and protections depend on the compiler and architecture.\nIn an unsafe hypothetical program, copying a longer string into a smaller fixed array can overwrite neighboring bytes. If control data are corrupted, execution may crash or be redirected. The explanation is a local memory-safety mechanism, not an instruction to attack a service.\nPrevent with bounds checking and safer APIs, memory-safe languages where practical, compiler stack protection, nonexecutable memory and ASLR. These defenses complement correct code; stack layout alone does not demonstrate a working exploit.','An out-of-bounds write can corrupt adjacent data or control information.','stack-overflow.png')
 a('2(e)','Five malware types','Virus: attaches to a host file/program and spreads when that host runs. Worm: self-propagates between systems. Trojan: poses as useful software while hiding harmful behavior. Ransomware: denies access to data or systems and demands payment. Spyware: covertly collects information about activity or data. These categories can overlap within one malicious program.','Virus, worm, Trojan, ransomware and spyware.')
 a('2(f)','RSA signatures and correctness','Textbook setup: choose RSA modulus n=pq, public exponent e and private exponent d such that ed≡1 modulo phi(n), with suitable primes and gcd(e,phi)=1. Encode the message hash as representative h in the RSA domain. Sign s=h^d mod n. Verify v=s^e mod n and check it matches the correctly encoded hash of the received message.\nCorrectness: ed=1+k phi(n), hence s^e=h^(ed)≡h mod n; for representatives not coprime to n, the same result follows separately modulo p and q and then by CRT. In practical RSA signatures use a specified secure encoding such as RSA-PSS; raw textbook exponentiation is not a secure signature construction.','Sign the encoded hash with d; verify with e; RSA exponent identities recover the representative.')
 prob=1-math.prod((365-i)/365 for i in range(58))
 a('3(a)','Birthday paradox and hash collisions',f'Assume independent, uniformly distributed birthdays over 365 days and ignore leap days. For 58 people, no collision requires each new birthday to differ from all earlier ones: P(no match)=(365/365)(364/365)…(308/365). Therefore P(at least one match)=1−product(i=0..57)(1−i/365)={prob:.8f}, or {100*prob:.4f}%. This supports the paper’s approximately 99% statement; it is not exactly 99%.\nFor an ideal hash with N equally likely outputs and q independent distinct-message hashes, P(collision)=1−product(i=0..q−1)(1−i/N). When q is small relative to N, this is approximately 1−exp(−q(q−1)/(2N)). About 50% collision probability occurs near q≈sqrt(2N ln 2)=1.1774 sqrt(N). Thus an n-bit hash has generic collision-search scale 2^(n/2), rather than 2^n. Real nonuniform birthday patterns and nonideal hash functions need separate analysis.','58 uniform birthdays give about 99.12% match probability; generic n-bit hash collisions take order 2^(n/2) trials.')
 b=[int(x,16) for x in '10 07 1a 13 0e 23 17 03 2e 01 02 17 0d 0f 1a 09'.split()]
 def xt(x):return ((x<<1)^ (0x11b if x&128 else 0))&255
 def gm(x,k):return x if k==1 else xt(x) if k==2 else xt(x)^x
 M=[[2,3,1,1],[1,2,3,1],[1,1,2,3],[3,1,1,2]]
 def calc(state):
  shifted=[row[r:]+row[:r] for r,row in enumerate(state)]
  mixed=[[gm(shifted[0][col],M[r][0])^gm(shifted[1][col],M[r][1])^gm(shifted[2][col],M[r][2])^gm(shifted[3][col],M[r][3]) for col in range(4)] for r in range(4)]
  end=[[v^1 for v in row] for row in mixed];return shifted,mixed,end
 state=[[b[4*col+r] for col in range(4)] for r in range(4)];shift,mix,end=calc(state)
 def fmt(mat):return '\n'.join('['+' '.join(f'{v:02x}' for v in row)+']' for row in mat)
 altstate=[b[k:k+4] for k in range(0,16,4)];alt=calc(altstate)[2]
 a('3(b)','AES ShiftRows, MixColumns and AddRoundKey','The input is already after SubBytes. Use the standard AES column-major loading: state[r,c]=input[4c+r]. All displayed bytes are hexadecimal.\nInitial state rows:\n'+fmt(state)+'\n1. ShiftRows rotates rows 0,1,2,3 left by 0,1,2,3 bytes respectively:\n'+fmt(shift)+'\n2. MixColumns multiplies each column by the GF(2^8) matrix with rows [02 03 01 01], [01 02 03 01], [01 01 02 03], [03 01 01 02]. Addition is XOR; multiply by 02 means xtime modulo x^8+x^4+x^3+x+1; 03·v=(02·v) xor v.\nFor the first output byte: 02·10 xor 03·23 xor 02 xor 09 = 20 xor 65 xor 02 xor 09 = 4e. Repeat for every row of every column. Mixed state:\n'+fmt(mix)+'\n3. AddRoundKey XORs each byte with 01. Resulting state:\n'+fmt(end)+'\nSerialized in standard column-major order: '+' '.join(f'{end[r][col]:02x}' for col in range(4) for r in range(4))+'.\nLayout note: if your instructor intends the list to be written row-by-row instead of the AES standard, the alternate final state is:\n'+fmt(alt)+'\nThe paper does not explicitly define loading order, so the standard is the main answer and the alternative is disclosed. This is the output of these specified round operations, not a full AES encryption from plaintext.','Standard AES column-major final bytes: '+' '.join(f'{end[r][col]:02x}' for col in range(4) for r in range(4))+'.')
 y=pow(2,25,59);c1=pow(2,4,59);shared=pow(y,4,59);c2=8*shared%59;inv=pow(shared,-1,59)
 a('3(c)','ElGamal public key, encryption and decryption',f'Given p=59, generator g=2, Bob’s private exponent x=25 and message M=8. Compute y=g^x mod p=2^25 mod59={y}. Public parameters are (p,g,y)=(59,2,{y}); if the question calls it a public-key pair, (g,y) is the pair with modulus p understood.\nWith ephemeral k=4: c1=g^k modp=2^4 mod59={c1}; shared key s=y^k modp={y}^4 mod59={shared}; c2=M·s modp=8×{shared} mod59={c2}. Ciphertext=(c1,c2)=({c1},{c2}).\nBob computes s′=c1^x modp={c1}^25 mod59={shared}. Its inverse modulo59 is {inv}, since {shared}×{inv}≡1. Recover M=c2×(s′)^−1 mod59={c2}×{inv} mod59=8.\nCorrectness: y^k=(g^x)^k=(g^k)^x=c1^x, so multiplying by the inverse cancels the mask. These tiny numbers are for the exercise only.','Public (59,2,'+str(y)+'); ciphertext ('+str(c1)+','+str(c2)+'); decryption recovers 8.')
 a('4(a)','Authentication versus authorization','Authentication checks who a claimant is, for example by verifying a password plus a second factor. Authorization checks what an authenticated principal may do, such as allowing a teacher to update their own course but not another teacher’s records. A valid login does not imply permission to every resource.','Authentication verifies identity; authorization decides permitted actions.')
 a('4(b)','Six evidence sources for forensics','1. Storage files and filesystem metadata. 2. Volatile memory and running-process state. 3. Operating-system and security logs. 4. Network captures and network-device logs. 5. Application artifacts such as browser history or email. 6. Mobile/cloud records such as provider audit logs and backups. Acquire lawfully, preserve integrity and record source, time and chain of custody; volatile evidence may disappear on shutdown.','Disk, memory, OS logs, network data, application artifacts and mobile/cloud records.')
 a('4(c)','Salted-password search space','A 2-byte salt has 2^16=65,536 possible values. A dictionary of 1,000,000 candidate words combined with every possible salt gives 1,000,000×65,536=65,536,000,000 word–salt combinations. This is the all-salt precomputation space under the question’s apparent interpretation.\nImportant distinction: a normal stored salt is public. For one chosen account with its salt already known, the attacker needs only the 1,000,000 dictionary guesses, each hashed using that known salt. Salting prevents reusable precomputation across salts; it does not add secret-password entropy.','All-salt space: 65,536,000,000 combinations; one known-salt account: 1,000,000 guesses.')
 a('4(d)','Polymorphic versus metamorphic virus','Polymorphic malware changes its encoded/encrypted representation and often its decryptor between copies while retaining the underlying payload logic. Metamorphic malware rewrites its own code into functionally equivalent forms, for example changing instructions or rearranging independent blocks, without relying merely on an encrypted wrapper. Both aim to frustrate simple byte-signature detection; behavior and structural analysis remain useful.','Polymorphic changes representation/wrapper; metamorphic rewrites equivalent code.')
 a('4(e)','Interpret drwxr----x','Read as d | rwx | r-- | --x. It is a directory. Owner: read, write and execute. Group: read only. Others: execute/search only. Octal permissions are 741. For a directory, read lists names, execute permits traversal/search and write permits modifying entries, usually requiring execute as well. Group members without execute cannot normally access entries by name through that directory.','Directory; owner rwx, group r--, others --x; mode 741.')
 a('4(f)','Rule-based versus statistical IDS','Rule/signature-based IDS matches observed events to explicit known patterns or rules. It is explainable and effective for covered behaviors but may miss novel variants or poorly specified cases. Statistical/anomaly IDS models expected behavior and flags deviations; it can expose unfamiliar activity but may produce false alarms when legitimate behavior changes. Rules can also express policy violations, and anomaly detection is not automatically proof of an intrusion.','Rules detect specified patterns; statistical models detect departures from a baseline.')
 a('4(g)','Two DHCP-based attacks','DHCP starvation exhausts the available address pool using many requests, disrupting legitimate client configuration. A rogue DHCP server supplies unauthorized network settings, potentially redirecting clients through a malicious gateway or resolver. Defend with DHCP snooping/trusted server ports, appropriate rate limits, port controls and monitoring.','Address-pool starvation and rogue DHCP responses.')
 a('4(h)','HTTP GET versus POST','GET requests retrieval of a resource representation and is defined to be safe and idempotent; query parameters commonly appear in the URL. POST asks the resource to process submitted content, commonly in the request body, and is not generally idempotent. POST is not automatically confidential: use HTTPS for both. Avoid placing secrets in URLs because they can appear in history and logs, and use CSRF protection for relevant state-changing browser requests.','GET retrieves; POST submits for processing. Both require HTTPS for confidentiality.')
 a('5(a)','Digital signature and its properties','A digital signature is generated with a private signing key and checked using its corresponding public verification key. It binds a message to the key holder under the scheme’s assumptions. Desired properties include message integrity, origin authentication, public verifiability and resistance to forgery; changing the message should invalidate the signature. It can support nonrepudiation when identity, key custody, timestamps and evidence are managed appropriately. It does not itself encrypt or hide the message.','Private-key signing and public verification support authenticity and integrity.')
 a('5(b)','Smurf reflection/amplification mechanism','Conceptually, a spoofed ICMP echo request appears to originate from a victim and is sent to a network that permits directed-broadcast replies. Multiple hosts reply to the victim, producing reflection and possible amplification. The diagram shows the mechanism rather than an operational attack procedure.\nDefend by disabling IP-directed broadcasts, blocking inappropriate broadcast echo responses, filtering spoofed source addresses and monitoring/rate-limiting abusive traffic. Reflection depends on vulnerable network behavior; it is not a feature every modern network permits.','Spoofed victim source + broadcast request → many reflected replies to the victim.','smurf-flow.png')
 a('5(c)','Adware and spyware with flow diagrams','Adware displays advertising and may be bundled with other software; its behavior ranges from consented advertising support to unwanted or deceptive conduct. Spyware covertly monitors and collects activity or sensitive data without appropriate consent. The two can overlap when advertising software secretly tracks people.\nThe diagram contrasts advertising display with covert collection/transmission. Reduce risk through trusted installation sources, meaningful consent, restricted permissions, endpoint protection and removal of unwanted applications.','Adware serves advertising; spyware covertly collects information.','adware-spyware.png')
 a('5(d)','Establishing a client–server SSL/TLS connection','The question uses SSL terminology; obsolete SSL is not appropriate for deployment. A conceptual modern TLS 1.3 handshake is: client sends ClientHello with supported parameters and key share; server selects parameters and returns ServerHello/key share; the server supplies its certificate and proof of key possession plus Finished; the client validates identity and handshake, then sends Finished. Shared secrets are derived into separate traffic keys, and application data uses authenticated symmetric encryption.\nThe certificate’s hostname, trust chain and validity must be checked. Optional client authentication adds client credentials. Older textbook TLS/SSL handshakes can use different message ordering and RSA key transport; do not mix those with this TLS 1.3 diagram.','Negotiate, authenticate server, derive keys and exchange encrypted authenticated data.','tls-flow.png')
 a('5(e)','Static versus dynamic analysis','Static analysis examines a suspicious file without running it: inspect hashes, strings, metadata, imports and code structure. It is safer to begin with but obfuscation can hide behavior. Dynamic analysis runs the file in a controlled isolated environment and observes process, filesystem, registry and network behavior. It can reveal runtime actions but may miss delayed or environment-aware behavior. Use snapshots, restricted networking and an appropriately isolated lab; never run an unknown file on a working machine merely to inspect it.','Static inspects without execution; dynamic observes behavior during isolated execution.')
 a('5(f)','How DNSSEC counters cache poisoning','A signed zone publishes signatures over record sets. A validating resolver checks the record signature using the zone DNSKEY and validates the delegation chain through DS/DNSKEY relationships to a configured trust anchor. A forged record cannot normally produce a valid signature, so a bogus signed answer is rejected rather than accepted into cache.\nThe diagram shows the chain of trust and the validation decision. DNSSEC supplies data-origin authentication and integrity, including authenticated denial for properly signed negative answers; it does not encrypt DNS traffic or guarantee that an unsigned domain is secure. It also does not fix a compromised legitimate signing key or malicious authorized zone content.','Validate signed DNS data through a trust chain; reject bogus signatures.','dnssec-flow.png')
 a('6(a)','RSA: private key, ciphertext and decryption','Given p=11, q=3, e=7. n=pq=33; phi(n)=(11−1)(3−1)=20. Find d with 7d≡1 mod20: d=3 because 7×3=21=1+20. Public key (e,n)=(7,33); private exponent d=3.\nMessage M=31≡−2 mod33. Cipher C=31^7 mod33=(−2)^7 mod33=−128 mod33=4. Decrypt M′=C^d modn=4^3 mod33=64 mod33=31, matching M.\nHandwritten calculations on the photograph are not an answer key; the modular powers are computed from the printed data. Tiny textbook RSA is for arithmetic practice and lacks the padding and sizes of a secure implementation.','d=3, n=33, C=4; decrypted message=31.')
 a('6(b)(i)','HTTPS/TLS session for data transfer','HTTPS carries HTTP inside an authenticated encrypted TLS channel. Resolve and connect to the intended hostname, negotiate TLS, validate the server’s certificate and handshake proof, derive session traffic keys, and then exchange HTTP requests/responses protected by authenticated encryption. The server typically proves possession of a private authentication key; bulk data uses symmetric session keys.\nThe TLS diagram illustrates version 1.3. HTTPS protects the connection to the validated hostname; it does not guarantee that a site’s content or business is honest, nor protect data after a compromised endpoint decrypts it.','HTTP over a validated TLS connection uses symmetric authenticated encryption for data.','tls-flow.png')
 a('6(b)(ii)','Session hijacking and mitigations','An attacker impersonates a logged-in user by obtaining or fixing an authenticated session identifier. Possible causes include exposed cookies, XSS, insecure transport, predictable tokens or failure to rotate an identifier after login.\nUse HTTPS throughout; high-entropy tokens; Secure, HttpOnly and appropriate SameSite cookies; rotation after authentication/privilege changes; idle and absolute expiry; server-side invalidation on logout; XSS prevention and reauthentication for sensitive actions. CSRF protection prevents a different class of unauthorized action and remains useful, but is not a substitute for protecting session tokens.','Protect, rotate, expire and revoke unpredictable session identifiers.')
 a('6(c)(i)','URL obfuscation example','URL obfuscation makes a destination look like a trusted site through misleading text, lookalike hostnames, encoding or user-info syntax. Safe illustrative example: https://trusted.example@evil.example/login has hostname evil.example; trusted.example is user-info before @. Another example is trusted.example.evil.example, which is under evil.example, not trusted.example. All example domains are reserved for documentation.\nInspect the actual parsed hostname, use trusted bookmarks and password-manager origin matching, and avoid relying on link display text alone. HTTPS to the wrong hostname still encrypts a connection to that wrong hostname.','Misleading URL text hides the actual destination; inspect the true hostname.')
 a('6(c)(ii)','SQL injection and prevention','SQL injection occurs when untrusted input is concatenated into SQL and changes its structure. In a toy login query built as SELECT * FROM users WHERE name=\'<input>\', an input containing a quote and SQL syntax can change the predicate rather than remain a name value. This illustrates the flaw without targeting a service.\nUse a parameterized query such as SELECT * FROM users WHERE name=? and bind the value separately. The database then treats the value as data, not executable SQL. Use allowlists for identifiers that cannot be parameters, least-privilege database accounts and safe error handling. Store passwords with an appropriate password hash and verify them correctly; query safety does not replace password security.','Separate SQL code from values with bound parameters; never concatenate untrusted input.')
